Reading: gain caps are a local-degree impairment. They show exactly when small optima need high-gain extension events, which is the construction analogue of keeping higher overlap orders in LRC.

Tournament Analysis over impairment techniques
technique | score | note
--- | --- | ---
observed direction-support Helly | 23 | small exact witnesses use few named direction families
direction-dropout spectroscopy | 22 | ablate one unit-direction pair and measure shadow price
gain-cap ladder | 21 | local extension degree as an overlap-order budget
obstruction-first extension solver | 19 | attach totally-unfaithful filters before expensive embedding search
automorphism-canonical beam | 16 | remove duplicate carrier states before scoring children
raw wider Moser beam | 11 | more states without new side-channel information
triangular-only construction | 10 | useful sanity baseline but too impaired for n=22 one-bit frontier
graph-only dense enumeration | 8 | too much geometry forgotten before the hard obstruction
score histogram: {8: 1, 10: 1, 11: 1, 16: 1, 19: 1, 21: 1, 22: 1, 23: 1}
directed 3-cycles: 0
Hamiltonian path count in this score quotient: 1
Challenged assumption: do not use points as the only tournament vertices. For method development, vertices should be impairments, side channels, direction families, gain events, and obstruction filters.

Technique proposals generated by the impairment lab
- Impaired-carrier spectroscopy: deliberately drop one direction, one gain level, or one side channel and measure the exact small-size failure mode.
- Direction Helly certificates: prove a candidate dense core needs only a small named subset of unit-direction pairs, then reattach the omitted directions as repair lanes.
- Shadow-price ledgers: rank directions by dropout loss, not just by usage in one witness cluster.
- Gain-order sieves: treat gain-4 and gain-5 extension events like higher overlap orders; a proof route should isolate them before raw enumeration.
- Obstruction-first beams: score children by edge gain plus proximity to known totally-unfaithful filters, not by edge gain alone.

